        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90007</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Counterfeit consciousness methods are increasingly applied to oncology datasets, but their clinical generalizability remains uncertain. We developed a prediction model using routine clinicopathologic variables. <bold>Methods: </bold>A retrospective cohort of 412 patients was divided into training and validation sets. A neural organization was trained to estimate 12-month progression risk. Calibration and discrimination were assessed in the validation cohort. <bold>Results: </bold>The neural organization achieved an area under the curve of 0.78. Performance decreased in patients with uncommon metastatic sites and incomplete laboratory data. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Counterfeit consciousness may support risk stratification when accompanied by external validation and clinician oversight. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
